{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Deborah, Rebekah's nurse, died</b> — I do not know why this verse was inserted between \"and he called the place El Bethel\" and \"and Elohim appeared to Jacob again,\" interrupting a single matter that took place at one time and in one place. For when Jacob arrived at Luz, which is Bethel, he built an altar and called the place El Bethel, and Elohim appeared to him there and blessed him. Why then was this statement placed in the midst of one continuous narrative?\n\nNow the most probable explanation is what our Sages said (Bereshit Rabbah 81:5), that it alludes to the death of Rebekah, and therefore he called the name of that place Allon Bacuth, for there would be no weeping and lamentation over the old nurse such that the place would be named for it. Rather, Jacob wept and mourned for his righteous mother, who loved him and sent him there, and who was not privileged to see him upon his return. Therefore Elohim appeared to him and blessed him to comfort him, just as He did for Isaac his father after the death of Abraham (Genesis 25:11). And in both cases the Sages said (Sotah 14a): \"He comforted mourners.\" Support for this is what is said below (Genesis 35:27): \"And Jacob came to Isaac his father at Mamre, Kiryat Arba\" — had Rebekah been there, it would have mentioned \"to his father and to his mother,\" for she was the one who sent him there and brought about all his good fortune, and Isaac at her advice commanded him to go there.\n\nRashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 35:8): \"Because they concealed the day of her death, so that people would not curse her — the womb from which Esau emerged — therefore Scripture did not publicize her either.\" This is a midrash of the Sages (Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer, zekhor 4). Now, the death of Leah is also not mentioned, but their intent there is because he mentioned the death by allusion and hung the matter upon her nursemaid; yet since Scripture speaks of her, why did it conceal the matter and not reveal it? And the reason of the curse is not clear, for Scripture did mention it concerning the death of Isaac (Genesis 35:29): \"and Esau and Jacob his sons buried him.\"\n\nBut it is possible to say that she had no honor in her death, for Jacob was not there, and Esau hated her and would not come there, and Isaac's eyes were dim and he did not leave his house; therefore Scripture did not wish to mention that the sons of Heth buried her. And something like this I found in Devarim Rabbah in the portion of \"When you go out to war\" [Tanchuma, Tetse 4]: they said — \"You find that when Rebekah died, they said: Who will go out before her? Abraham is dead; Isaac sits in the house and his eyes are dim; Jacob has gone to Paddan Aram; shall Esau the wicked go out before her? And they will say: the burial of this one is a disgrace, for from her he nursed. What did they do? They brought out her bier at night. Rabbi Yossi ben Chanina said: Because they brought out her bier at night, the verses did not state her death explicitly but only obliquely, as it is written: 'and he called its name Allon Bacuth' — two weepings; as it is written: 'and Elohim appeared to Jacob and blessed him' — with what did He bless him? He blessed him with the blessing of mourners\" — end of quote. Now, because Esau was alone at her burial, they feared the curse and saw that there would be no honor for her, and this is the meaning of the allusion.\n\nNow Deborah was with Jacob, for after she came with Rebekah she had returned to her land, and now she came with Jacob to see her mistress. Or she had been occupied with raising Jacob's children for Rebekah's sake and out of love for her, and she was in his house. And it is possible that she is not the nurse mentioned regarding her (Genesis 24:59): \"and they sent Rebekah their sister and her nurse,\" but rather a different nurse who remained with Laban and Bethuel, and now Jacob brought her with him to provide for her old age, in honor of his mother — for so is the way of the distinguished to have many nurses. For it is far-fetched that the old woman would be the emissary whom his mother would send to Jacob, according to the words of Rabbi Moses the Preacher."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "Ramban cites the Aramaic passage from the midrash very literally; I have rendered it in a register that preserves the colloquial flavor of the original Aramaic while keeping it intelligible in English. The citation 'פסדרכ זכור ד' is resolved as Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer; the citation 'אלה הדברים רבה' (Devarim Rabbah) is identified by Ramban himself as also found in Tanchuma Tetse 4. The phrase 'ברכת אבלים' is the rabbinic term for the blessing given to mourners, here left in Hebrew with the context making the meaning clear."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:55.346114330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}